On Tue, 2 Sep 2003, Boudewijn Waijers wrote:
> I am a programmer myself (SAP / ABAP), and I flatly refuse to use any
> program that cannot be operated with just the keyboard.

A keyboard offers a much more diverse choice of absolute input methods,
whereas the mouse only has a few buttons and the two-dimensional movement.
For most program inputs the keyboard is and will always be superior to a
mouse, because key values are not context-sensitive: Typing Alt-F always
results in Alt-F, whereas using the mouse pointer to click on an area
in a menu bar requirer the user to perform different actions depending on
where the pointer currently is. 

(This is not based on any scientific study, just my view on why this
debate.)

> If the mouse is only needed in some cases, it's no problem, since this
> actually makes your movements more diverse. Windows, however, tries to use
> the mouse for everything, which is not only unpractical, but even
> tiresome.

Windows has enough flaws that you don't need to make them up. The first
action that I perform with my mouse when I start Windows is to move the
pointer down to the lower right corner, so that it vanishes from the
desktop. From then on, I only use the mouse to navigate in the occasional
program where it is necessary, but this need hardly arises outside of
games. 

(In fact I often don't have the option anyway, because the batteries of my
mouse run out frequently (it's optical and wireless (a gift)).)

